---
layout: article
title: Endpoint checks
description: Check the availability of HTTP, DNS, TCP, and SMTP endpoints on a schedule with Appwrite Cloud synthetic monitoring.
---

Endpoint checks, also called synthetic checks, are available on paid Appwrite Cloud plans. Appwrite connects to your configured target on a schedule and opens an alert when the check fails.

# Create an endpoint check {% #create-an-endpoint-check %}

1. In your project, open **Observe** > **Monitors**, then click **Create monitor**.
2. Choose **HTTP**, **DNS**, **TCP**, or **SMTP** as the type. Under **Target**, enter what to check and any expectations for that type.
3. Under **Checks**, set how often to check, from 30 seconds to 1 hour, and how long a check may take before it times out, from 5 seconds to 1 minute. Your plan sets the shortest interval you can choose, and the timeout can't be longer than the interval.
4. Choose recipients under **Notify** and pick a **Severity**. To set a name or change the cooldown, expand **Name, cooldown**.
5. Click **Create**.

The **Monitors** list shows the latest result, latency, and time of each check. Open a monitor to see its status, target, last result or latency, and when it last fired.

# HTTP {% #http %}

Enter an `http://` or `https://` URL and choose a request method. You can add headers and, for methods that send one, a request body. Enter a comma-separated list of **Expected status codes**, or leave it empty to accept any 2xx response.

For an HTTPS URL, use **Certificate expiry warning** to set how many days before expiration to alert. For example, check `https://api.example.com/health` with **GET**, expect status `200`, and set a warning 14 days before the certificate expires.

# DNS {% #dns %}

Enter the domain and select an **A**, **AAAA**, **CNAME**, **MX**, **TXT**, or **NS** record. You can set a **Resolver** to query and an **Expected answer**. Leave **Expected answer** empty to accept any answer for the selected record type.

# TCP {% #tcp %}

Enter a host and port. The check succeeds when Appwrite can establish a TCP connection to that port within the timeout. Use an HTTP or SMTP check when you also need to verify how the service responds.

# SMTP {% #smtp %}

Enter the mail server's host and port. Appwrite checks whether the server accepts a session and can complete the configured TLS handshake.

| TLS setting | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| **From the port** | Use implicit TLS on port 465, STARTTLS on port 587, and a plain connection on other ports. |
| **None** | Connect without TLS. |
| **Upgrade with STARTTLS** | Open an SMTP session and upgrade it with STARTTLS. |
| **Implicit TLS** | Start TLS as soon as the connection opens. |

# Create with a server SDK {% #create-with-a-server-sdk %}

Each type has its own method: `createHttpMonitor`, `createDnsMonitor`, `createTcpMonitor`, and `createSmtpMonitor`. Create an [API key](/docs/advanced/platform/api-keys) with the `monitors.write` scope to call them. This example checks `https://api.example.com/health` every minute, expects status `200`, and fails 14 days before the certificate expires.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');

const monitors = new sdk.Monitors(client);

const monitor = await monitors.createHttpMonitor({
    monitorId: 'api-health',
    name: 'API health',
    url: 'https://api.example.com/health',
    statusCodes: [200], // optional
    certificateDays: 14, // optional
    interval: 60, // optional
    timeout: 10 // optional
});
```

```server-python
from appwrite.client import Client
from appwrite.services.monitors import Monitors

client = Client()
client.set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
client.set_project('<PROJECT_ID>')
client.set_key('<API_KEY>')

monitors = Monitors(client)

monitor = monitors.create_http_monitor(
    monitor_id = 'api-health',
    name = 'API health',
    url = 'https://api.example.com/health',
    status_codes = [200], # optional
    certificate_days = 14, # optional
    interval = 60, # optional
    timeout = 10 # optional
)
```

